      "id": "theverge-2026-07-30-nasa-s-curiosity-rover-found-a-sea-of-polygons-on-mars",
      "title": "NASA’s Curiosity rover found a ‘sea of polygons’ on Mars",
      "summary": "The latest discovery from NASA's Curiosity Mars rover is a field of honeycomb-shaped polygons covering a Martian valley called Valle Grande. As Gizmodo reports, Curiosity has snapped pictures of the unusual terrain texture before, called polygonal fractures, each about 1.5 to 3 inches wide, but NASA says it's previously only found them in small patches, \"nothing on the scale discovered in Valle Gr",
      "body": "The latest discovery from NASA’s Curiosity Mars rover is a field of honeycomb-shaped polygons covering a Martian valley called Valle Grande. As Gizmodo reports, Curiosity has snapped pictures of the unusual terrain texture before, called polygonal fractures, each about 1.5 to 3 inches wide, but NASA says it’s previously only found them in small patches, “nothing on the scale discovered in Valle Grande.”&nbsp;\nCuriosity captured a 360-degree panorama of the “sea of polygons” on June 19th and 20th (Martian sols 4,930 and 4,931) that shows the texture spreading out in all directions. Project scientist Ashwin Vasavada says the Curiosity team “measured their shapes and chemistry carefully and are hopeful there are clues in the data as to how these features formed.”&nbsp;\nNASA says billions of years ago, “lakes and streams dappled the lower foothills of Mount Sharp, a 3-mile-tall (5-kilometer-tall) mountain that Curiosity has been ascending since 2014.” This terrain could also indicate there was once water in Valle Grande, based on previous polygon patches:\n> Some of the polygons that the mission has spotted in the past clearly formed as mud cracks, though a variety of processes can contribute to their honeycomb textures, including cycles of warm and cold temperatures or compression that squeezed water out of the sediment when the surface was buried.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "NASA",
        "News",
        "Science",
        "Space"
      ],
      "author": "Stevie Bonifield",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T17:20:06.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/nasa-curiosity-sea-of-polygons.jpeg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/science/973314/nasa-curiosity-rover-mars-polygons",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-30-virgin-engineer-died-after-explosion-in-fuel-tank",
      "title": "Virgin engineer died after explosion in fuel tank",
      "summary": "Georgie Buxton was injured after a \"non-combustion explosion\" inside the fuel tank.",
      "body": "A Virgin Atlantic engineer died of a traumatic brain injury after an explosion as he worked inside a plane fuel tank, an inquest heard.Aircraft technician Georgie Buxton, 23, was injured during the incident at Heathrow Airport on 11 June and underwent various surgeries to his head and neck. He died at St Mary's Hospital on 7 July.Senior coroner Lydia Brown opened and suspended an inquest into his death at West London Coroners' Court on Thursday, as she is waiting to see if any criminal charges are brought.A criminal investigation has been launched by the Metropolitan Police who are working with the Health and Safety Executive. The coroner said she had no details of any potential charges.Buxton was one of two Virgin Airlines' technicians who were examining a fuel leak on a Boeing 787 900 on 11 June at about 08:45 BST.A spokesman for Aviation Policing told the court: \"Both technicians wore breathing apparatus to protect them from any residual fuel fumes whilst inside the fuel tank.\"The spokesman said there was \"a non-combustion explosion\" while Buxton was inside the fuel tank.He added: \"Georgie Buxton took the impact of that explosion to his head and torso causing him to lose consciousness and suffer catastrophic injury.\"A forensic post-mortem examination, carried out at the request of the police, found Buxton had suffered a traumatic brain injury – a craniofacial trauma.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T17:20:01.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5161/live/75dfe330-8c08-11f1-8665-e382dc49dfdb.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cz059e4d7xzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
