      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-30-rise-reforming-yc-s26-is-hiring",
      "title": "Rise Reforming (YC S26) Is Hiring",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.ycombinator.com/companies/rise-reforming/jobs/wJ9Q9nv-senior-chemical-process-engineer\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49112683\nPoints: 0\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "We turn waste gases into valuable supply-secure chemicals Senior Chemical Process Engineer$125K•Chicago, IL, US / Wheaton, IL, USRoleEngineering, Chemical engineeringConnect directly with founders of the best YC-funded startups.Apply to role ›About the roleWe’re looking for a process engineer with 5+ years of experience in scaling catalytic technologies to pilot and beyond. You will play a central role in designing, constructing, and commissioning our pilot unit and subsequently designing our first commercial unit.\nProcuring, integrating, operating, and fixing pilot-scale equipment;\nPilot chemical plant commissioning and operation;\nExperience with gas-to-liquids processes (syngas-to-methanol preferred);\nCollaboration with EPCs in overseeing FOAK commercial unit design;\nExperience with Aspen or other process simulation software.\nExperience working with modular systems;\nExperience working in a fast-paced startup environment.\nAs Rise Reforming scales, this role is expected to grow into a VP of Engineering or Chief Engineer position, leading a team of process and project engineers across multiple commercial deployments. Senior Chemical Process Engineer will report to the CTO.\nCompensation: $125,000+/year + stock options + performance bonus + benefits (health insurance, 401k).\nLocation: In person, Chicago, IL and Wheaton, IL.\nAbout Rise ReformingGeopolitical conflicts and natural disasters frequently expose the vulnerability of the American chemical industry. The reason? Production is centralized and fossil fuel-dependent. The solution? Localized production using an undervalued resource: biogas. The US produces enough biogas to make over $20 bn/y worth of chemicals, but 60% is wasted in low-margin applications or flared altogether.\nRise Reforming is commercializing a modular process to transform this underutilized biogas on-site into cheap, supply-secure chemicals like dimethyl ether (DME) and methanol. We're doing our pilot project at a Chicagoland wastewater plant. This will be the first wastewater biogas-to-methanol demo in the Americas. Join us!",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
