      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-30-ftc-sues-hims-hers-for-allegedly-sharing-patients-medical-da",
      "title": "FTC sues Hims & Hers for allegedly sharing patients’ medical data with advertisers Meta and Snap",
      "summary": "The U.S. federal consumer watchdog said Hims & Hers, which prescribes for sexual wellness and mental health conditions, used website trackers to share customers' information with advertisers.",
      "body": "The Federal Trade Commission is suing healthcare giant Hims & Hers for allegedly sharing its customers’ medical and healthcare information with advertisers and tech giants, like Meta and Snap, as well as for misleading consumers about its privacy practices.\nThe lawsuit is the federal consumer watchdog’s latest crackdown in recent years on healthcare companies that share sensitive information with outside companies without their customers’ knowledge. Hims & Hers, now a publicly traded company, provides prescription medication for sexual wellness, mental health conditions, weight loss, and other issues, and as such handles a large amount of sensitive patient data.\nCompanies typically try to learn more about their customers by installing code on their websites in order to share users’ information with advertisers, like Meta and Snap, which then use the data to provide information about who is visiting their websites and when.\nThe FTC also accused Hims & Hers of deceptive billing, and drawing up policies that allegedly made it difficult for customers to cancel, in violation of federal consumer protection laws.\nThe FTC has previously taken action against telehealth startup Cerebral, alcohol recovery provider Monument, as well as data giant GoodRx and therapy provider BetterHelp. These companies were similarly accused of sharing patients’ sensitive data through their websites to third-party tech giants and advertisers.\nThe use of pixel-sized trackers has previously revealed how people’s sensitive data gets shared with the companies who provide the code, highlighting how misconfigurations can result in unwanted data collection.\nIn 2024, TechCrunch found that the U.S. Postal Service was sharing logged-in users’ home addresses with Meta, LinkedIn, and Snap by using their pixel tracking code. The USPS removed the code soon after.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nHe can be reached via encrypted message at zackwhittaker.1337 on Signal. You can also contact him by email, or to verify outreach, at zack.whittaker@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Zack Whittaker",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-30T13:30:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/NYSE-hims-hers-shot1.jpg?resize=1200,674",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/30/ftc-sues-hims-hers-for-allegedly-sharing-patients-medical-data-with-advertisers-meta-and-snap/",
