      "title": "How Old Is Ann?",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://quuxplusone.github.io/blog/2026/07/29/how-old-is-ann/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49108971\nPoints: 11\n# Comments: 3",
      "body": "From “Topics of the Times,”\nNew York Times of 1924-11-17:\nThe amount and quality of intelligence utilized in the working out of cross-word\npuzzles almost precisely equal those that were revealed some years ago when an\namazing — and discouraging — army of Americans filled the newspapers that cater\nto such folk with triumphant solutions of an arithmetical problem concerning the\nage of a supposititious Ann — a problem that any eighth-grade boy or girl who is\n“all there” should have solved in his or her head in about thirty seconds.\nThe older I get, the more sense it makes when the phrase “some years ago” turns out\nto refer to something from two decades back. The problem, apparently first published\nin the New York World circa 1903-10-09,[1]\nMary is 24 years old. She is twice as old as Ann was\nwhen Mary was as old as Ann is now. How old is Ann?\n(A writer to the Montgomery (Alabama) Advertiser of 1903-10-24\nthat you can get the apparently-most-common wrong answer if you read the puzzle\nincorrectly as “She was twice as old as Ann was when Mary [sic] was as old as Ann now is.”)\nThe New York Evening Post (quoted in the Princeton (Minn.) Union)\n“Wherever it may have originated — and this is itself a matter of\ncontroversy — the problem concerning the ages of Ann and Mary\nhas caught the country’s fancy as few such things have since\n(1904-02-20): “The question ‘How Old Is Ann?’ has been dramatized\nand was staged at White Plains. Barney Gerard, the author, makes Ann a mischievous\nlittle girl, who refuses to go to school. The school authorities get after her to\npunish her but must first find out how old she is. Complications result.” •\nThe New York Evening World\n(1903-11-17): “ ‘How Old Is Ann?’ is the title\nof a new song which Frank Daniels sang last night at the Victoria\nTheatre. When the opening lines sounded the audience settled back in its seats with\na sigh of relief. ‘At last,’ it meant just as if it was expressed in words,\n‘we are going to find out how old that cussed sister of Mary is.’ But they didn’t,\nand there’s where the fun came in. The song has a rattling chorus and a catchy tune.” •\nfingered Ann as the cause of the\nThe World of 1903-11-07 “revealed”\nthe perpetrator of the problem as one “Mr.\nPerkin Warbeck, of Newark, N.J.”\n“the answers of a few leading Philadelphians.”\n“I don’t see why that simple question is puzzling anybody. I\nknew Ann is 12 years old as soon as I saw the problem,” said\n“There is no other solution, I tell you. Look here, I’ll explain\nit to you. Mary is 24 years old. She is twice as old as Ann —\nthat’s a cinch. Of course, then, Ann is half Mary’s age, or 12\nyears. There ought to be a comma after ‘Ann,’ though.”\nIt was suggested to Mr. Shattuck that there was no comma in the problem.\n“Doesn’t make any difference,” replied he promptly.\n“Let’s see — twice as old as Ann ‘was.’ That ought to be ‘is’ —\nas Mary was when Ann is. I’m not mixed up. I tell you, Ann is 12.”\nNot to be outdone, the New York Evening World published\nthe answers of “many prominent men of New York,” including:\nPoliceman Bert Gallagher: “Ann is old enough to know better\nthan not tell her age when she knows how much trouble her silence is causing.\nShe ought to be ashamed.”\nJohn T. Meehan: “Ann was ninety years old. I know, because\nshe and her brother shook hands with Lafayette on his visit to New York in 1824.\nOld Peter was the steward of the beanery for forty years, and he was ninety-two when\nhe died; Ann was three years younger. He and Ann used to sell newspapers on the\nVesey Street corner during Jackson’s first term. Why, Ann was— Oh, that World\nquestion about the girl who’s twice as old as herself? I haven’t time to fuss\nwith that. I thought you were talking about old Peter Rose’s sister Ann.”\n“Just twice as old as she was when Mary was twice as old as Ann. That’s easy.\nSix years ago Mary was eighteen and Ann was thirteen. To-day Mary is twenty-four\nand Ann is nineteen. Figure it out for yourself. Don’t you see that if Mary\nis twice as old as Ann was when Mary was— I mean twice as old as Mary when Ann\nwas half as old as Mary, consequently— but there, let the professors figure it\nout. It’s as bad as ‘when is a mice when it spins.’ ”\nThe Wyoming Tribune of 1903-10-28 calls it\nAnyone could figure it out if they would stop to think. Ann is 16, of course,\nbecause if Mary is twice as old as Ann was, all you have to do is to find out\nwhen. Now she is 24, Ann’s age was 12, or twice six. Mary is now four times six,\nwhich being divided by six, gives four. Add this to twelve, and there you are. —Smith.\nWhen Mary had a little lamb, she was twice as old as Ann was when Ann was as old\nas Mary now claims to be. How old is the sheep? —James Barton Adams.\nA correspondent for the Kansas Journal\nAnn is sixty-three years of age. Obviously,\nAnn is as old now as Mary was when Mary was\nhalf as old as her present age less the age of Ann\nat the time she was half as old as Mary was when\nshe was twice as old as Ann is, unless you figure\nthat Ann was half as old as if she were twice\nMary’s present age, less Ann’s age when she was\ntwice as old as Mary is now, or twenty-four.\nTwenty-four and fifteen are thirty-nine, and\ntwenty-four and thirty-nine are sixty-three, which\nrepresents Mary’s present age subtracted from half\nof Mary’s age at the time she was half as old as\nAnn. I would not solve this problem for any one\nThe Washington Times of 1903-10-27\nI used to be contented and took comfort in my life,\nI dwelt in deep placidity, removed from care and strife,\nI never worried as I jogged along my even way,\nI worked a bit, I slept a bit, and still had time for play;\nI never had such things as nerves; my brain was clear and cool,\nBut now I am a nervous wreck, and soon will be a fool.\nIt came about in this wise: One fell day a genial friend,\nWhose mind is full of whist and chess and problems without end,\nPropounded in a guileless way a problem which, to scan,\nSeemed simple: All you had to do was find the age of Ann.\nNow, Ann is Mary’s sister, which you’ve no doubt heard before,\nAnd Mary— it is impolite, but Mary’s twenty-four.\nNow, Mary’s twenty-four, and she is twice as old today\nAs Ann was when her sister was — that is, of course, to say,\nWhen Mary was — as old as Mary’s sister Ann is now.\nWhich brings the question up to you: What’s Ann’s age, anyhow?\nThat’s what my friend propounded, and I answered, at a pinch,\n“The problem you propound, sir, is dead easy — it’s a cinch.”\nI worked all day upon it, and I also worked all night,\nBut somehow that confounded thing would never come out right:\nAt first I figured it that Ann was twelve, then eight, then ten,\nAnd then I twisted it around and worked it out again.\nThis time I learned, to my dismay, that figures disagree:\nThat although Mary’s twenty-four, her sister’s sixty-three!\nI raved and swore; my hair I tore; I vented all my spleen\nUpon some luckless wight who said that Ann was sweet sixteen.\nI’ve worried nights, I’ve worried days, I cannot eat my meals;\nI jabber those confounded words until my poor brain reels;\nIf this thing keeps up many moons, good-bye, old world — farewell!\nFor me ’twill be the welcome grave, or else the padded cell.\n(A less entertaining, but more democratic, sampling of “Ann” correspondence\nin the Evening World of 1903-11-11.)\nThe craze was short-lived. By 1903-12-21 “Prof. Josh M. A. Long”\nof the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Humor was\nin the World for any information as to\n“the whereabouts of ‘How Old Is Ann?’ Has been missing about ten days.\nRecently released from the Old Jokes’ Home, last heard of in Harlem.”\nFootnote: Or in the New York Press of 1903-10-12, signed “John Mahon,”\nif you believe a column from 1937\nSouth Hill (Va.) Enterprise 1937-12-23).\nOr maybe the Press of 1903-10-16\nBut the true story, as relayed\nin a Scrap-Book article titled “The Vicissitudes of a Problem”\n(August 1906) is that “How old is Ann?” first appeared in the Newark Evening News of 1903-04-21,\nheadlined “Stories of the Town.”\nThe author was one Robert D. Towne;\nand when the meme didn’t catch at first in Newark, he tried again, Lampton says,\nby sending it to the Philadelphia North American and signing it “Perkin Warbeck.”\nTowne certainly let it be known that he was the “Ann” man\nearning his obituary a spot among Time magazine’s\nNow hear this, from the Sequachee Valley (Tenn.) News of\nA man is twice the age his wife was when he was the age she is now.\nWhen she reaches his present age, their combined years will be 100.\nFind the age of each.",
