      "id": "bbc-2026-07-29-serial-killer-given-whole-life-orders-for-murders-of-two-men",
      "title": "Serial killer given whole life orders for murders of two men as police investigate four more deaths",
      "summary": "Police are reviewing four unexplained deaths at a homeless shelter where James Desborough stayed.",
      "body": "Serial killer given whole life orders for murdering two menImage source, Devon and Cornwall PoliceImage caption, James Desborough was on Tuesday found guilty of murdering two men he met at a homeless shelterByJohanna Carrat Winchester Crown Court and Julia GregorySouth WestSerial killer James Desborough has been given two whole life prison sentences with no chance of parole - as police revealed they are looking into four unexplained deaths at the homeless shelter that housed him.Desborough, already serving a minimum of 19 years for the murder of his prison cellmate, was given the whole life tariffs for killing 57-year-old Claudio Aquilino and Daniel Coleman, 43, in Cornwall.Aquilino and Coleman met Desborough at Newquay's Sandy Lodge homeless shelter, where police are now reviewing the unexplained deaths of two other men and two women.Sentencing, Mr Justice Pushpinder Saini described Desborough, 40, as \"a highly dangerous individual who had no regard for the life of those you think are worthless\".Desborough befriended the two vulnerable men and used \"extreme violence\" on both of them, before dismembering and concealing their bodies, the judge said. He said the former builder used their bank cards and made journeys to give the impression they were still alive.Image source, Devon and Cornwall PoliceImage caption, The remains of Daniel Coleman and Claudio Aquilino were found in Cornish woodland Their remains were discovered close to Desborough's woodland cabin near Sticker in Cornwall.It was while he was on remand charged with their murders that he strangled his cellmate, Steven Kempster, at HMP Exeter.The judge at Winchester Crown Court said the offences had an exceptionally high level of seriousness and it was rare to impose such a sentence with no chance of parole.Desborough was also sentenced to five years on each count of preventing the lawful burial of Aquilino and Coleman, who were last seen alive on 18 April 2025 and on 3 June 2025 respectively.Figure caption, Police arrest triple murderer James Desborough in his woodland cabinIn victim impact statements read out in court, Coleman and Aquilino's families spoke of their anguish imagining the pain and fear the men suffered.Coleman's brother, Tony Nye, said there \"weren't enough words in the English language\" to describe the suffering Desborough had caused his family.\"You have taken from this world my big brother in the most disgusting and horrific way,\" he said.\"The world will see you for the vile degenerate coward you are. There will be no forgiveness for you in this world or the next.\"His mother, Sandra Coleman, said: \"You took my firstborn son. You destroyed any hope of us coming together as a family. \"You have given me a life sentence of pain, despair, anxiety and hopelessness.\"Image source, Devon and Cornwall Police/PA WireImage caption, Police found items belonging to both men hidden in Desborough's woodland cabin Aquilino's uncle David Bonomi said his nephew was a \"friendly, talented artist\" who had been killed in a \"brutal, cruel and evil manner\".He said the family had reported him missing as it was \"very unusual for him not to have been in contact\" as he called his mother every day.Speaking earlier about the review of the four unexplained deaths, Det Supt Rachael Bentley, from Devon and Cornwall Police, said: \"As a major crime investigation team we were informed by the coroner's officer for His Majesty's Coroner that there were four deaths at Sandy Lodge.\"As part of the investigation, a detective inspector, separate to the investigation but within major crime, did a review of those deaths, and that included commissioning some more forensic work, some CCTV, and statements.\"Inquiries were ongoing and Desborough was \"not a suspect at this time\", Bentley said.\"We don't believe there is a link at this time, but I said it's an ongoing investigation,\" she added.Image source, Devon and Cornwall PoliceImage caption, Police found bone fragments and body parts in woodland near Desborough's cabinBentley said police attended each of the deaths at Sandy Lodge and inquiries suggested natural causes but coronial investigations were paused when Desborough was arrested for murder.Desborough dismembered Coleman and Aquilino's bodies, tried to burn their remains and hid them in woodland in Sticker.As part of a lengthy search of dense laurel and bogland involving about 100 officers, police found thousands of bone fragments in a stream and body parts in a shallow grave.Several handwritten notes were also found inside the cabin, including one headed \"Bug out list\", another \"Hostiles\" and a diagram labelled \"Kill Zone\".Image source, Devon and Cornwall PoliceImage caption, Handwritten notes were found inside the cabinClosing the case to the jury, Ahmed Hossain KC, said Desborough was capable of switching to \"very serious violence\".Bentley described the killings as \"callous and cold-blooded\", as \"both men were vulnerable and trusted James Desborough as their friend\" before he brutally murdered them and \"savagely desecrated their bodies\".Desborough killed Kempster in Exeter Prison on 15 September 2025, just three days after they started sharing a cell.The court heard Desborough was irritated by his cellmate's snoring and returning of unwashed cutlery.The night before the murder he warned he would kill him if he continued to snore.Follow BBC Cornwall on X, external, Facebook, external and Instagram, external. Send your story ideas to spotlight@bbc.co.uk, external.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
