      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-i-turned-down-love-island-and-won-silver-at-the-commonwealth",
      "title": "I turned down Love Island and won silver at the Commonwealth Games",
      "summary": "Katie Shanahan was offered the chance to appear on the dating show last year - but decided she wanted to inspire a new generation of young swimmers.",
      "body": "Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Katie Shanahan claimed silver in the 200m breaststroke on the opening night of the Games.ByJonathan GeddesGlasgow and west reporterIn another reality, swimmer Katie Shanahan might have been looking for love on her favourite TV show - but instead made a decision that would lead her to a silver medal at the Commonwealth Games.The 22-year-old told BBC Scotland News she was offered the chance to appear on Love Island last year, the reality dating show where singletons are placed in a Spanish villa.However Glasgow-born Shanahan turned down the offer of sunshine in Mallorca, choosing instead to focus on her sport at the Tollcross swimming centre.Last Friday she claimed silver in the 200m backstroke on the opening night of the Games. Despite her sporting success, she says would not be averse to a trip to Casa Amor in the future.\"I'm the biggest Love Island fan ever, I've watched every season\", she laughed.\"Last year the producers asked me and Abbie Wood [the English swimmer] if we would be interested in doing it.\"I couldn't go on though, because of focusing on the swimming, but who knows in the future? I was a bit tempted but the swimming had to come first.\" Love Island, which concluded its 2026 series earlier this week, has aimed to attract a number of athletes to the programme.Northern Ireland boxers Jon McConnell and Kaci Rock progressed through initial auditions for the show, but then had to drop out to focus on their sport as the Games drew closer.A number of lower league and non-league footballers have also appeared on the often controversial dating show over the years.  Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Shanahan has been inundated with well-wishings since her performance on FridayHowever Shanahan's decision paid off in the pool, and she was seen fighting back tears after securing silver on Friday night.Having been inspired by seeing Hannah Miley in action at the 2014 Games, Shanahan told BBC Scotland News she hoped future generations might have be likewise inspired by watching her in the pool this time around.\"I've always said that if I can inspire the next generation of athletes coming through, then my job has been done,\" she explained.\"I know after 2014 watching Hannah I felt inspired, and it would be nice to think that maybe there's some younger girls watching the 200m backstroke this time, and maybe they'll get into swimming from it.\"She joked that the main problem since last Friday was finding the time to get back to various friends and well-wishers sending her messages to congratulate her.  \"I'm so grateful for the support, and with it being a home Games it feels like that support has been ramped up a bit, which is so nice to experience.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T22:10:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/9957/live/b4fce9e0-8aba-11f1-9997-a1a12b7801e6.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cg4dve90w90o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-28-ariana-grande-is-suing-the-hackers-who-8217-ve-been-leaking-",
      "title": "Ariana Grande is suing the hackers who&#8217;ve been leaking her songs and videos for years",
      "summary": "Ariana Grande filed a lawsuit on Monday in the Los Angeles County Superior Court against the currently unidentified hackers who allegedly stole and leaked private content. It aims to \"uncover the identities of these currently unknown and unscrupulous individuals,\" referred to as  \"John Doe 1 and John Does 2 through 100.\" The lawsuit also says that \"In 2023 alone, 45 unreleased songs\" were \"hacked,",
      "body": "Ariana Grande filed a lawsuit on Monday in the Los Angeles County Superior Court against the currently unidentified hackers who allegedly stole and leaked private content. It aims to “uncover the identities of these currently unknown and unscrupulous individuals,” referred to as&nbsp; “John Doe 1 and John Does 2 through 100.” The lawsuit also says that “In 2023 alone, 45 unreleased songs” were “hacked, stolen, and leaked,” adding that hundreds of similar leaks have happened since Grande’s music debut in 2011.&nbsp;\nThe stolen content is said to include “still-in-production, unreleased masters and demos, footage from recording sessions, music videos, behind-the-scenes photographs and videos, as well as album and photoshoot outtakes.”&nbsp;\nThe lawsuit accuses the unidentified hackers of selling the content, as well as publishing it on social media platforms like X, TikTok, and YouTube. It claims their actions are invading her privacy and violating California’s Comprehensive Data Access and Fraud Act, a law against unauthorized computer access that has come up in cases including Sony’s lawsuit against George Hotz and Meta’s lawsuit against the Pegasus spyware.\nAs reported earlier by Variety, the lawsuit claims the hackers have repeatedly targeted people who work with Grande. In one incident in 2019, hackers stole Grande’s photos from a photographer’s Dropbox account. The following year, unreleased footage was stolen from a producer’s hacked “mobile device.” In 2024, scammers used phishing emails to get unreleased photos from a technician working with one of Grande’s photographers.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Music",
        "News",
        "Security",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Stevie Bonifield",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T21:48:50.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/gettyimages-2255276905.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.736367339261,100,78.527265321477",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/972233/ariana-grande-hacking-lawsuit",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-28-bot-detection-startup-spur-nabs-200m-from-insight",
      "title": "Bot-detection startup Spur nabs $200M from Insight",
      "summary": "Spur Intelligence has raised a $200 million round from Insight Partners for its tech that can identify legit human traffic from bots.",
      "body": "2:29 PM PDT · July 28, 2026\nImage Credits:Malte Mueller / Getty Images\nSpur Intelligence, a cybersecurity startup based in Lake Mary, Florida, has raised a $200 million round led by Insight Partners.\nSpur, founded by two former Defense Department engineers in 2017 — five years before ChatGPT’s public launch — was prescient. The startup’s tech helps enterprises distinguish legitimate human users from increasingly well-hidden bot traffic to help identify fake users and threats.\n“As sophisticated criminal VPNs, residential proxy networks, and anonymization infrastructure proliferate, organizations are increasingly operating with a critical blind spot: they can see the activity, but not the infrastructure behind it,” Insight’s Thomas Krane said in a written statement.\nDetecting malicious traffic has, of course, been a hill corporate security teams have been climbing for eons. But nothing compares to the onslaught facing them today. As of mid-2026, bots are now more active on the internet than humans, Cloudflare reported last month.\n“Thought it would be end of 2027, then early 2027, but agentic traffic growing so fast that bots have now passed human traffic online for the first time in the Internet’s history,” Cloudflare founder and CEO Matthew Prince posted on X last month, pointing to his company’s latest traffic report.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Julie Bort",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T21:29:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/s.jpg?resize=1200,840",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/28/bot-detection-startup-spur-nabs-200m-from-insight/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-28-the-union-drive-at-the-wikimedia-foundation-is-expanding",
      "title": "The union drive at the Wikimedia Foundation is expanding",
      "summary": "In June, UK staff at the nonprofit that runs Wikipedia became the first to announce their intention to form a union. Now, US-based employees at the Wikimedia Foundation are joining the union drive, but management at the nonprofit has declined to voluntarily recognize their union.\n\"While the Wikimedia Foundation touts to the public that they plan to take a neutral stance in our organizing efforts, ",
      "body": "In June, UK staff at the nonprofit that runs Wikipedia became the first to announce their intention to form a union. Now, US-based employees at the Wikimedia Foundation are joining the union drive, but management at the nonprofit has declined to voluntarily recognize their union.\n“While the Wikimedia Foundation touts to the public that they plan to take a neutral stance in our organizing efforts, they have already proven that not to be the case behind the scenes,” a press release sent by Communications Workers of America, with which Wiki Workers United is affiliated, reads in part. “When the Foundation rejected our request for voluntary recognition this week, they immediately sent out to employees and the public statements couched in classic union-busting rhetoric, rhetoric undoubtedly provided by outrageously expensive union-avoidance law firms. This insulting and wasteful use of Foundation resources is both an insult to Foundation employees and to the Free Knowledge Movement as a whole.”\nIn a public statement, the foundation said it believes a secret ballot election with the NLRB is the “appropriate path forward.”\nThe unionization efforts come at a time when tensions are high between the foundation and the thousands of unpaid volunteers who edit, write, and maintain Wikipedia. In May, hundreds of Wikipedia editors threatened to go on strike after the foundation dissolved an engineering team that worked closely with volunteers. Some volunteers questioned whether the layoffs were related to union efforts, which the foundation denied.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Creators",
        "Labor",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
