      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-28-sam-altman-is-ready-to-decelerate",
      "title": "Sam Altman is ready to decelerate",
      "summary": "His change of position comes after \"the first security incident that I have felt very viscerally.\"",
      "body": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman says that it may be time to “pace” AI development so the world will be ready for it.\n“We may have to pace the rate of AI development to give ourselves enough time for society to harden around some of these new capability levels,” he told Patrick O’Shaughnessy, the host of the Invest Like the Best podcast, while also “trying to figure out how we do that in a way that does not feel like regulatory capture for anyone and also does not feel like collusion among the frontier labs.”\nBoth OpenAI and Anthropic came out in support of a petition circulated by employees at the frontier labs, calling on the US government to “support an international effort to develop the technical and governance tools needed to deliberately pace the frontier of automated AI development.”\nAltman has avoided signing on to past campaigns to slow AI progress, calling a 2023 open letter that proposed a similar slowdown “missing most technical nuance about where we need the pause.”\nApparently, he’s softened on the idea, thanks in part to the incident where one of OpenAI’s advanced models managed to break out of a secure computing environment and hack into Hugging Face, an online model database, using several zero-day exploits.\nOn the podcast, the OpenAI co-founder called it an “extremely sci-fi cyber incident…[t]his is the first security incident that I have felt very viscerally.”\nOpenAI researchers have paused training on that model while they work out how to keep their sandbox secure. But Altman said that as models become more powerful, the need to “pace” their development could become key to safe deployment.\nWhile model safety and alignment has always been a concern in the AI world, the arrival of Anthropic’s highly capable Mythos model earlier this year has turned hypotheticals into real-world problems.\nHowever, the industry has a trust problem, and challenging economics that give major players an incentive to play up the dangers of their systems in ways that experts disagree about — for example, whether or not Anthropic’s Fable model should have been briefly banned from use or not. Similarly, when Kimi K3, a large, open-weight model built in China, was released, OpenAI head of strategic futures Dean W. Ball said that it threatened the economics of frontier labs, making it difficult to separate their safety concerns from their financial interest.\n“I think a lot of the talk about safety concerns is well-founded, and then a lot of it is about people that just really, even if it’s slightly subconscious, want to concentrate power,” Altman mused, in what reads as a dig at his rival, Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, who signed the petition. “I am terrified of a world where the very real fears of AI are used as a way to say, ‘Only this small group of people can have it because it’s too dangerous, and only they understand it, but don’t worry, like, they’re gonna make the right decisions for all of us.’ I don’t believe in that.”\nStill, OpenAI has pushed back against efforts to develop government rules for AI models, instead preferring an industry-led approach where AI labs would create ostensibly independent organizations that would evaluate the security of models and the safety approach of their makers. The challenge for both that approach to regulation and any efforts to slow AI development will be getting the various players in the industry on the same page, whether they are rival frontier labs in the U.S. or competitors in China.\nThis story was updated to include OpenAI and Anthropic’s support of the “Pacing the Frontier” petition.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nTim Fernholz is a journalist who writes about technology, finance and public policy. He has closely covered the rise of the private space industry and is the author of  Rocket Billionaires: Elon Musk, Jeff Bezos and the New Space Race. Formerly, he was a senior reporter at Quartz, the global business news site, for more than a decade, and began his career as a political reporter in Washington, D.C.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Tim by emailing tim.fernholz@techcrunch.com or via an encrypted message to tim_fernholz.21 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Tim Fernholz",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T20:17:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/GettyImages-2226496284.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/28/sam-altman-is-ready-to-decelerate/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-07-28-pacing-the-frontier",
      "title": "Pacing the frontier",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.pacingthefrontier.com/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49089240\nPoints: 82\n# Comments: 72",
      "body": "AI could help create a dramatically better future, but that outcome is not guaranteed. The world's leading AI companies believe they could be close to automating AI research. It is hard to predict exactly how much this will accelerate AI progress, but there is a real risk that capability development rapidly accelerates beyond our ability to understand or control the resulting systems.To realize AI's potential, industry, government, and society at large may need the option to buy time to address emerging risks, develop security measures, and strengthen oversight. But each company—and country—is under intense competitive pressure not to unilaterally slow that acceleration. And today, the world lacks the technical and governance tools to deliberately pace frontier-wide progress.Building on work already underway to monitor frontier model releases:",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "reducesuffering",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T20:09:09.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.pacingthefrontier.com//pacing-the-frontier-preview.jpeg?v=4",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.pacingthefrontier.com/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-despair-to-delight-for-wales-golden-girl-evans",
      "title": "Despair to delight for Wales' golden girl Evans",
      "summary": "Gymnast Ruby Evans goes from despair to gold in Glasgow after glory in the floor final at the Commonwealth Games.",
      "body": "It has been a 14-year journey to this point.Evans started gymnastics at the age of five at her local leisure centre in Cardiff with her parents wanting to utilise her energy.\"She used to somersault off the window sills,\" said mother Leanne, while father Chris admitted they \"still did not know anything about gymnastics\".Evans was part of the British team that finished fourth at the Paris Olympics in 2024 - and was the youngest member of the Team GB squad at 17.In 2025, she won a silver medal on the floor at the World Championships in Jakarta.As well as her sporting success, she is also popular on social media with more than 117,000 Instagram followers.So Evans was one of the most recognisable faces in the Team Wales squad coming to Glasgow and under pressure to deliver success, although she was not comfortable with the poster girl image.\"I struggled a bit in camp,\" admitted Evans. \"It was like, 'Ruby Evans, Ruby Evans', so I knew there was a bit of pressure on me.\"You have to block it out as best as you can. I think I did do that.\"It has been quite a week for Evans and Welsh gymnastics in general with drama, disappointment and delight in equal measures.She was part of the Wales women's team that just missed out on a medal in the team event. The quintet had been part of the second rotation and led the competition before the final four teams performed.The television cameras kept panning to the Wales team in the crowd, who could only sit and wait to see if they had won a medal, before they were denied a bronze by England.Then Evans, alongside Emily Roper, went for individual all-around glory. She was second after two of the four disciplines before she had a terrible beam routine, which included a faltering start and falling off halfway.That dropped her to seventh and only a miracle on the floor would see her rise back to the top three.Evans ended up sixth. It left her distraught and you hoped that would not be the defining image of her Games.She pulled out of the uneven bars on Monday as she rested a shoulder injury.\"I just watched the competition, had some food and chilled and watched the Love Island final,\" said Evans.She watched the 20-year-old Roper identical twins provide one of Wales' stories of Glasgow so far in winning bronze medals within a couple of hours of each other.Abigail beat Emily to the the third podium position in the vault. Emily brushed aside the disappointment of finishing fourth to return to claim bronze in the uneven bars, with the iconic image of her sister cheering her from the stands.A first Welsh women's vault for 32 years, a first bars medal in history. And immense exposure for the 20-year-old twins they never expected, with numerous interview requests for them and their parents from around the world.The focus switched to Evans on the final day of competition with two chances of medals.The watching Wales supporters included chef de mission Gethin Jones and Team Wales captain Olivia Breen, the Para-sprinter who won silver on Monday night.First it was the beam. The nemesis from the all-around individual final. Evans came out to a big roar and produced the trademark dragons symbol of the Welsh team before bursting out laughing. She seemed relaxed. A welcome sign. It was a much more solid performance as she finished seventh. An expected result.This was just the warm-up before the main event an hour later when the floor competition began. The word had come that the outfit would not be just the traditional red, white and green of Wales but something sparkly and it did not disappoint.Evans was the final one of the eight competitors to perform. Would it be a case of saving the best until last?England's Abigail Martin was first and set the standard. Six others tried and failed to topple her. Then it was the turn of Evans as her golden costume almost blended into the floor.There was a hush as she delivered one of the most impressive performances of her short, but glittering, career so far.Evans stated how she used anger as motivation in her signature discipline. It worked.\"I feel like I perform better when I have a bit of fire in me,\" she said.\"It's like a completely different feeling. I went out there like my dragon was absolutely firing.\"But was it enough? Nobody was quite sure. The Roper twins were pictured on the big screen cheering. They, like everybody in the Welsh pockets of the stands, hoped so.The wait was agonising. Finally the verdict was in. Wales had its first gold medal. Evans could not quite believe it.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T20:00:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/8ee0/live/343b51a0-8ac1-11f1-a7ab-8b30adf0800a.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/gymnastics/articles/c3r088n0wlwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
