      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-28-whatsapp-now-lets-you-make-calls-using-its-web-app",
      "title": "WhatsApp now lets you make calls using its web app",
      "summary": "Similar to the smartphone and desktop apps, the web app will support calling features such as screen-sharing and reactions.",
      "body": "7:53 AM PDT · July 28, 2026\nFor years, WhatsApp calls have been supported only on the smartphone app or its native desktop app. Now Meta is finally letting users make and receive audio and video calls using WhatsApp for Web.\nSimilar to the smartphone and desktop apps, the Calls tab on the web interface will house your call history and favorites. The web app will also support calling features you get in the desktop app, such as screen-sharing and reactions.\nWhatsApp is rolling out a few more updates for calls as well. Users can now transfer calls from one device to another without hanging up, so if you initially received a call on desktop or on the web, you can continue speaking on your phone.\nThe company is also introducing waiting rooms. If someone has a link to a group call, you can let them in when you want to, or boot them out. This is a key update for people who might host work calls on WhatsApp. When creating a call link, you can enable the “Require approval to join” option to create a waiting room for the meeting.\nAnother small but useful upgrade is background noise suppression, which filters out noise in large places. The company said WhatsApp will start streaming video calls in high definition in the first few seconds of the call. Before now, calls would sometimes take a few seconds to switch to high-definition video.\nWhatsApp recently opened up reservations for usernames. Once the username feature goes live, people will be able to use them to call other users across platforms.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Ivan Mehta",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T14:53:22.000Z",
