      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-the-10-most-overcrowded-commuter-trains-revealed",
      "title": "The 10 most overcrowded commuter trains revealed",
      "summary": "Overall, overcrowding in England and Wales fell slightly last year due to increased capacity, the government says.",
      "body": "The most overcrowded train service in England and Wales last autumn was a Thameslink morning rush hour service between Bedford and Three Bridges in West Sussex, according to the Department for Transport (DfT).Second on the list was a West Midlands service from London Euston to Crewe, Cheshire, departing at 17:46, the government figures reveal. Overall, they show overcrowding on commuter trains serving major English and Welsh cities fell slightly, for the first time since 2020, due to an increase in capacity.Only 1.1% of peak-time travellers were on an overcrowded train, the DfT said. More than 1.2 million of the average 1.9 million journeys on a typical autumn day were in London.The DfT says work is under way to improve capacity where there are pinch points during peak hours.The Thameslink service which topped the list had a \"load factor\" – meaning how full the train was – of 176% at London St Pancras shortly after 08:00.The West Midlands service to Crewe had a load factor of 170%. It was busiest when it departed Euston.The third most crowded train was a Transport for Wales service from Bridgend to Rhymney, with a load factor of 161%. It was busiest at Llanishen.Four of the top ten services were operated by South Western, and Thameslink appeared twice more.Thameslink: Bedford to Three Bridges via London St Pancras (176% at 08:09)West Midlands: London Euston to Crewe (170% at 17:46)Transport for Wales: Bridgend to Rhymney via Llanishen (161% at 16:45)South Western: Alton to London Waterloo (159% at 08:22)Thameslink: Bedford to Three Bridges via London St Pancras (155% at 08:24)South Western: Fareham to London Waterloo via Vauxhall (151% at 08:19)Thameslink: Epsom to London Bridge (150% at 08:28)South Western: Portsmouth Harbour to London Waterloo via Vauxhall (149% at 08:27)South Western: London Waterloo to Basingstoke via Vauxhall (147% at 17:50)West Midlands: London Euston to Birmingham New Street (146% at 17:56)The DfT figures were based on passenger counts taken on Tuesdays, Wednesdays and Thursdays between September and December 2025, and represent travel on a typical autumn weekday.Overcrowding fell slightly overall compared with autumn 2024, with an average of 16,800 (1%) passengers a day travelling in excess of capacity.The DfT said seating capacity has grown 4% in the past year.A spokesperson said: \"The vast majority of passengers – 98.9% – typically travel on services that are not overcrowded.\"Where there are pinch points during peak hours, work is under way to improve capacity. GTR has added carriages to its busiest commuter services, while South Western Railway has boosted capacity by nearly 30% in the last year with new trains. \"Bringing track and train together under Great British Railways will support further improvements, helping create a joined-up service that puts passengers first.\"A quarter of passengers travelling in and out of London at peak times had to stand. But in other cities, only 5% of passengers could not get a seat.A Thameslink spokesperson said its trains were designed to have ample standing room on board its busiest commuter routes.\"We are always monitoring how busy our services are and continually make changes to provide more space.\"In the last few years alone, we've lengthened trains on some of our busiest Bedford to London services and introduced an additional peak-time service to provide more choice and extra space.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T14:17:57.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7a89/live/72a40b80-8a7b-11f1-82f1-411b076ec7a9.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c20dxevjlvxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-can-burnham-s-vision-for-manchester-s-schools-really-work-fo",
      "title": "Can Burnham's vision for Manchester's schools really work for the rest of England?",
      "summary": "The prime minister's plans for technical education have their roots in the Manchester Baccalaureate.",
      "body": "Two years ago, I stood at the back of a conference room in Manchester Hall, sipping a coffee and watching Andy Burnham unveil his vision for transforming technical education in Greater Manchester.He had announced plans for the Manchester Baccalaureate, or MBacc, the year before, and, armed with PowerPoint slides and guest speakers, was running through the ins and outs in front of a room full of education and business leaders.Now, as prime minister, Burnham has announced plans to make that idea national, and overhaul the technical education system across the whole of England.The details are still scarce at this stage. We know it will involve giving pupils the option to study more vocational courses and take up work experience from Year 10, but how it will work in practice is still up in the air.One thing that is certain is that the thinking behind it, and the ambition, is the same as that which underpins the MBacc: a determination to give teenagers a clear, step-by-step route into more practical and technical jobs.The trouble is, the MBacc itself can be quite a difficult concept to get your head around if you're not familiar with it.Let's be clear from the start: it's not another type of qualification, like the T-levels and V-levels announced in England in recent years.Instead, it's a framework that aims to give teenagers a \"clear line of sight to good jobs\" in vocational and technical areas.The best way to think about it is a step-by-step route - picture a flow chart - that teenagers can follow from school into any given technical career. It's set up in parallel to a more clearly defined academic route. During that conference event in July 2024, Burnham and other speakers made clear that those who want to go on to desk jobs can follow a well-trodden path of GCSEs, A-levels, and a university degree.But for those who want more practical careers, the landscape is much more complicated. The MBacc is best seen as an effort to simplify that landscape, spelling out for teenagers who want to go into more vocational or technical careers which courses they can study, and ensuring they have the opportunity to study them along with hands-on work experience.It spells out routes to careers in seven different categories:Construction and the green economyCreative, culture and sportDigital and technologyEducation and early yearsFinancial, business and professional servicesHealth and social careManufacturing and engineeringIts success is difficult to measure, since the MBacc is still in its infancy. It's not until 2030 that it seeks to achieve its mission of guaranteeing every young person a clear path to good jobs. By 2035, the aim is for every young person to have 45 days of work experience by the time they're 19.According to the most recent data, the MBacc has been rolled out across 101 secondary schools and colleges in Greater Manchester - about 40% of the total. The Greater Manchester Combined Authority says 1,405 young people accessed workplace experiences during its second year.Time will tell, as those teenagers leave school and enter the world of work, how transformative it will be.And what works in Greater Manchester may not work everywhere else. The prime minister has stated explicitly that he wants to expand the approach across England. \"I created the Greater Manchester Baccalaureate or MBacc... to bridge the gap between our schools and our employers,\" he wrote in the Times. \"Now we're taking that idea national.\"It may well be scalable. The seven MBacc \"gateways\" - although designed specifically around Greater Manchester's \"fastest-growing industries\" - are actually quite broad-ranging. They mirror some of the areas already targeted by efforts to boost technical education at a national level. There are T-levels in construction areas as well as in education and early years, for example.But there will be challenges along the way. The prime minister is clear that he wants technical and vocational education to be tied to local economies. He plans to work with local leaders, employers and schools, to \"identify the industries driving growth in local areas and make sure that local kids have a clear route into them\".If that's to be the case, he will need to make sure that teenagers in all parts of England - including in areas without thriving industries - don't fall through the gaps.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T14:03:55.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ab36/live/17ee5aa0-8a8b-11f1-83c5-cf36fe4b083e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c79gevrvgddo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-28-fish-audio-raises-52m-seed-to-build-ai-voice-models-for-crea",
      "title": "Fish Audio raises $52M seed to build AI voice models for creators and enterprises",
      "summary": "Since launching last year, the startup today has more than 8 million people using the open source or hosted version of its models, and now generates annual recurring revenue of $21 million.",
      "body": "The market for AI-generated voice models is massive. Creative use cases require AI voice models to be more expressive, while enterprises looking to automate customer support and sales ops need them to be more steerable.\nPalo Alto-based Fish Audio wants to cater to all of those use cases with its library of more than 15,000 natural language controls. Since launching last year, the startup now has more than 8 million people using the open source or hosted versions of its models, and generates annual recurring revenue of $21 million.\nTo continue building on that traction, the startup on Tuesday said it has raised $52 million in a seed round that was led by Coreline Ventures and Capital Today. The funding also saw participation from 359 Capital, Parable, Play Time, Alphalist Partners, Bayhouse Ventures, Carya Venture Partners, and HF0.\nFish Audio started as a small project by former Nvidia researcher Shijia Liao, who, frustrated by non-expressive synthetic voices available on the market, trained a voice-generation model on a single GPU, which he then open-sourced. The Fish Speech repository on GitHub now has more than 31,000 stars and is used by indie developers, video game designers, and creators.\nThe company has launched five models in the last year: four speech-generation models and one speech-to-text model. It has open-sourced three of its speech-generation models, but its latest S2.1 Pro model is available only through its paid API.\nFish Audio offers paid monthly plans suited for creators and teams that unlock a set number of minutes of generation plus voice-cloning features. The company also offers an enterprise version of its APIs and platform, and says organizations like HeyGen and Sanas are already using it.\n“Every enterprise has different use cases and different preferences. For example, companies like HeyGen, which use our voices to power AI avatars, want realism in voices; a gaming studio would want expressive voices for their characters; and voice agent companies like LiveKit want more natural-sounding and low-latency voices that are expressive enough for calls,” Fish Audio’s CEO and co-founder Rissa Cao said.\nOne way the startup has built its library of voices is by asking users to submit their own voices for training its models, and compensating them if their voices are used. That resulted in some trouble a few months ago, however, as some creators alleged that their voices were uploaded to Fish Audio without their consent. The startup had a DMCA takedown process in place to address such concerns, but the takedowns themselves took a long time.\nCao told TechCrunch that the company has now automated the takedown process. Creators can submit a short voice sample or a contract to prove that an uploaded voice belongs to them, and their voice will be taken off the startup’s platform in less than three minutes, she said.\nStill, that doesn’t prevent anyone from uploading an artist’s voice without their knowledge. And until the artist finds out, their voice will continue to be used on the platform unless they file for its removal.\nOsuke Honda, a partner at Coreline Ventures, said a community-driven model only works when creators trust the platform.\n“A community-centric approach can only become a durable advantage if creators trust the platform. That means consent, transparency, and attribution must be built into the product rather than treated as afterthoughts. I believe the industry needs to move toward verified voice ownership, clear licensing terms, easy reporting and takedown processes, and eventually revenue-sharing models where creators benefit financially when their voices are licensed or used commercially,” he said.\nCao said when the startup was only offering its product as an open source project with plans for creators, it was running efficiently and didn’t need outside capital. But it wanted to develop more advanced models and wanted to accommodate enterprises as investor interest was ramping up, which led it to seek capital.\nLooking ahead, Fish Audio plans to release an audio understanding model this year. It’s also building a speech-to-speech model.\nThe speech-generation market is crowded, with companies like ElevenLabs, WellSaid, Cartesia, Speechify, Async (previously Podcastle), and Krisp competing for creators and enterprises’ budgets.\nAccording to Rico Mallozzi, a partner at 359 Capital, fine-grained controls for developers and cost-efficient model training will help Fish Audio compete better with big AI labs.\n“I think what they’ve been able to build, state-of-the-art models, with the team they have, compared to some of these other well-funded AI labs or companies, is incredible. It shows their technical acumen in closing the gap between artificial-sounding and human-like voices,” Mallozzi told TechCrunch over a call.\nThe story has been updated to reflect that the company raised $52 million in the seed round.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.",
