      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-rain-brings-hope-of-relief-for-crews-fighting-cairngorms-wil",
      "title": "Rain brings hope of relief for crews fighting Cairngorms wildfire",
      "summary": "Firefighters continue to tackle hotspots in large areas of smouldering moorland in the Highlands.",
      "body": "Image source, Ronan Dugan/WildLand LtdImage caption, Firefighters and volunteers have been tackling the wildfire after it broke out on 15 JulyBySteven McKenzieHighlands and Islands reporterThere is hope that heavy rainfall will help efforts to tackle a wildfire that has been burning in the Cairngorms for two weeks.About 70 firefighters and nine fire appliances remain at the scene, 13 days after the start of the fire that has burned through about 10 sq miles (25 sq km) - and saw some homes in Nethy Bridge evacuated at the weekend.Sandy McCook, Nethy Bridge Community Council chairman, said it was hoped rain that has been falling since Monday night would make a \"huge difference\".The Cairngorm National Park Authority (CNPA) said progress was being made in tackling the wildfire, but added helicopters would likely continue to water bomb hotspots on Tuesday.CNPA said people should avoid the Glenmore, Abernethy and Loch Morlich areas because they were unsafe due to the firefighting operation.Days of dry and windy weather have made conditions challenging for fire crews and volunteers tackling flames burning across peatland moors and in woodland.McCook said residents in Nethy Bridge hoped a change in the weather would help efforts to tackle remaining hotspots.He said: \"The overnight and continuing rain today will undoubtedly make a huge difference to the firefighting.\"McCook added: \"There is a distinct smell of smoke around but that will be from the rain and lack of wind.\"The community council has helped to raise more than £50,500 from donations to cover the costs of meals for firefighters and volunteers.Image source, Steve Kenyon Roberts/Deeside Gliding ClubImage caption, An image taken on Friday by Steve Kenyon Roberts of Deeside Gliding Club of the wildfireCNPA said the Scottish Fire and Rescue Service (SFRS) reported on Monday evening there had been a reduction in visible flames, but warned large areas were still smouldering.The park authority said the fire was still burning deep in the peat soil.It added that helicopters, sourced and funded by Forestry and Land Scotland, would likely continue water bombing on Tuesday, assisted by drones equipped with thermal cameras to detect hotspots.The fire broke out near the village of Glenmore, outside Aviemore, on 15 July and led to residents and campsites being evacuated.At its height, more than 500 firefighters were involved in the response and Police Scotland declared it a \"major incident\" on Friday.Image source, SatVuImage caption, A satellite image taken on Monday morning of areas of the wildfire An image of the wildfire was captured by SatVu's HotSat-2 satellite at 04:39 on Monday.SatVu said the picture showed fire fronts moving north in the Abernethy National Nature Reserve, one of the area's affected by the incident.The company said that behind the most intense \"flaming zones\",the fire had left a trail of burning vegetation.Figure caption, Aerial images show scale of Cairngorms fireSome local groups have been critical of ministers for being slow in scaling up the response to the fire.On Monday, Scotland's justice secretary denied trying start a political row after rejecting an offer of UK military support to tackle a major wildfire in the Cairngorms.The UK government said it would provide military helicopters for heavy lifting, along with support from stand-by battalions, after a major incident was declared at the weekend.But Neil Gray, whose ministerial responsibilities include the fire service, said it had been unable to fulfil a \"specific request\" for water-bombing helicopters and that firefighters on the ground had the resources which were needed.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T11:31:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/36d0/live/c75d61d0-8a62-11f1-b329-01e04c1ebb9b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyqn7r5g91o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-only-use-var-when-error-blatant-referee-chiefs",
      "title": "Only use VAR when error blatant - referee chiefs",
      "summary": "Video assistant referees should only be used to change \"blatantly incorrect\" decisions and clear missed incidents, say European refereeing officials.",
      "body": "Video assistant referees should only be used to change \"blatantly incorrect\" decisions and clear missed incidents, say European refereeing officials.Chief refereeing officers from all 55 European national football associations met last week with Uefa refereeing officials and a representative from the International Football Association Board (Ifab) to address recent changes to the game's laws.Attendees said \"lengthy, microscoping reviews\" indicate VAR has gone beyond its original scope.When VAR was added to the laws of the game in 2018 by football's rulemakers Ifab, its \"philosophy\" was \"minimum interference - maximum benefit\", with the intention of reducing \"unfairness caused by clear and obvious errors or serious missed incidents\" relating to goals, penalties, direct red cards and mistaken identity. Uefa said that at its recent meeting that refereeing bosses agreed a return to those original aims is needed.\"It was reiterated that, in accordance with protocol, VAR must only intervene in situations of 'clear and obvious' error, ie. when a referee decision is blatantly incorrect or when the referee has missed a clear incident,\" Uefa said in a statement.\"VAR is not meant to re-referee the game, it is an essential support to the referees, who must remain at the centre of all decision-making.\"Lengthy, microscoping reviews are often a symptom revealing that the decision on the field was not clearly and obviously incorrect, and should therefore be limited.\"Officials acknowledged recent clarification from Ifab around the correct interpretation of the \"mistaken identity\" rule introduced before this summer's World Cup, which says that if a player is booked or sent off but the foul was committed by the opposition team the decision can be changed.Switzerland's Breel Embolo was the first player to be sent off as a result of the new law following a controversial second yellow card for simulation against Argentina. Leandro Parades originally received a yellow card for his challenge before VAR intervened.Ifab clarified that VAR can only review cautions to \"identify the player who committed the offence that was penalised, not to review or alter the offence\".There was also \"unanimous support\" for recent rule changes designed to reduce unnecessary delays, including time limits on goal-kicks, throw-ins and substitutions, and new regulations around on-field injuries and goalkeeper tactical timeouts.This statement of intent sounds positive.We all know what a 'clear and obvious error' is right? Luis Diaz's offside goal that wasn't for Liverpool in 2023 – a decision VAR should definitely have overturned. It wasn't, but that was down to human error not protocol.The problem is 'clear and obvious' is a subjective phrase.What one manager might think was the worst mistake ever, his counterpart in the other dugout might feel was a fair call.There has been a lot of debate around the amount of time taken to make decisions and if the time period passes a certain threshold, there should be no change.In the cold light of day, that seems sensible. But what if it is three decisions – starting with an offside, then a potential handball, before finally a foul – that might be certain. If the potential offside or handball could rule out the foul, they have to be looked at as well surely?It feels as though Ifab is trying to improve an imperfect situation and it should be applauded for that.Just don't expect it to end – or even minimise – the arguments around the merits of individual decisions.",
