    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-28-lyft-and-baidu-enter-london-s-robotaxi-battleground-as-testi",
      "title": "Lyft and Baidu enter London’s robotaxi battleground as testing begins",
      "summary": "Baidu's Apollo Go autonomous vehicles will be available on Freenow, the mobility network that Lyft acquired in 2025.",
      "body": "Chinese tech giant Baidu has started testing autonomous vehicles in London as part of its partnership with Lyft and Freenow, the German taxi and multi-mobility app that Lyft now owns. Baidu is the latest in a string of companies to test self-driving technology in the U.K. ahead of commercial robotaxi deployments.\nThe testing, which began Tuesday with human safety operators, comes nearly a year after the two companies struck a strategic partnership to deploy Baidu’s purpose-built Apollo Go RT6 robotaxi across key European markets through the Lyft platform. The vehicles will eventually be available through Freenow, which Lyft acquired in 2025 for about $197 million.\nThat deal gave Lyft a foothold in Europe’s ride-hailing market, where a handful of well-funded companies are now jockeying to be first to market with robotaxis.\nBaidu and Freenow by Lyft (as the latter service is now called) said they expect to invite the public to hail their robotaxis in 2027. The companies, which didn’t provide a more detailed timeline, noted that the launch will depend on regulatory approval.\nFor now, dozens of test vehicles will operate within London’s borough of Brent. Lyft and Freenow said they continue discussions with safety and city officials, including Transport for London (TfL) and the Centre for Connected and Autonomous Vehicles (CCAV). The U.K. government is in the process of creating autonomous vehicle regulations and opened applications in May for companies interested in an AV pilot program that lets companies test self-driving vehicles under government oversight.\nWhen the service does launch, Freenow by Lyft said it will operate a hybrid network — employing the same language rival Uber has used — meaning human drivers operating taxis and private-hire vehicles will work alongside the robotaxis.\n“As a platform with deep roots in the taxi industry, our priority is ensuring that autonomous technology supports the professional drivers who keep London moving,” Thomas Zimmermann, CEO of Freenow by Lyft, said in a statement.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nKirsten Korosec is a reporter and editor who has covered the future of transportation from EVs and autonomous vehicles to urban air mobility and in-car tech for more than a decade. She is currently the transportation editor at TechCrunch and co-host of TechCrunch’s Equity podcast. She is also co-founder and co-host of the podcast, “The Autonocast.” She previously wrote for Fortune, The Verge, Bloomberg, MIT Technology Review and CBS Interactive.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Kirsten by emailing kirsten.korosec@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at kkorosec.07 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Kirsten Korosec",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T08:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Freenow-by-Lyft-x-Baidu-Apollo-Go.jpg?resize=1200,900",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/28/lyft-and-baidu-enter-londons-robotaxi-battleground-as-testing-begins/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-champions-northampton-to-open-prem-season-against-newcastle",
      "title": "Champions Northampton to open Prem season against Newcastle",
      "summary": "Prem champions Northampton host Newcastle on the opening weekend of the 2026-27 season, with Exeter at home to Gloucester.",
      "body": "Champions Northampton will host Newcastle Red Bulls on the opening day of the 2026-27 Prem rugby season.Saints beat Exeter to lift the trophy for the second time in three seasons in June and start the new campaign at home on Friday, 25 September. The Chiefs are also at home for their first game, with Gloucester set to visit Sandy Park on the Saturday.Elsewhere across the opening weekend, Harlequins are at home to Bath on Friday, Sale host Bristol on Saturday evening and Leicester play Saracens in the only match on Sunday.The 2026-27 Prem final will be held on Saturday, 19 June 2027 at the Allianz Stadium, Twickenham, with the season to take a series of breaks around international fixtures.After five rounds in six weeks through September and October, there will be a four-week Prem break through November for the autumn matches of the Nations Championship.With European competition beginning in December there are four Prem rounds across six weeks until January, before a 10-week league pause again in February and March for the Six Nations.The new season also begins with promotion and relegation officially scrapped from the division. While no club has been relegated from the Prem or promoted up from the Champ since Saracens in 2020 and 2021, this has now been formalised as part of a series of changes to English rugby voted through in February, as the league moves towards a franchise-style model.A handful of matches next season will also be staged at major stadiums, as has become customary for some Prem clubs in recent years. Harlequins play their two annual 'Big Game' fixtures at the Allianz Stadium, starting against Northampton, with the Prem's first-ever Monday night fixture on 28 December over the festive period. They return to England's home stadium to play Newcastle in round 15 in May. Gloucester are hoping to return to Villa Park after playing an inaugural game in Birmingham last season, however the venue is still to be finalised.If all goes ahead they will again play Leicester in round 12 for the Slater Cup on the weekend of 27 March, while Bristol host the Cherry and Whites at Cardiff's Principality Stadium in a west country derby in round 13 three weeks later. \"There's huge momentum behind the Gallagher Prem heading into the new season, following a third consecutive sold-out final, significant international investment in our clubs, record broadcast audiences and soaring interest from younger fans,\" said Simon Massie-Taylor, chief executive officer of Prem Rugby.\"And following a landmark agreement to change our operating model earlier this year, we are well on the way with our plans to expand the league by the 2029/30 season,\" he added.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-28T07:35:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/8765/live/43538850-85c7-11f1-8aff-e16fe8f2ba64.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/rugby-union/articles/c2k7xkpqpexo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-28-i-just-found-all-the-classified-stuff-downstairs-biden-tells",
      "title": "'I just found all the classified stuff downstairs,' Biden tells ghostwriter in recording",
      "summary": "Recordings between Biden and his ghostwriter reveal references to classified information and memory gaps.",
      "body": "Audio recordings that Joe Biden fought for years to keep private - in which he acknowledges possessing classified material after leaving office as US vice-president - have been released by Republicans in Congress. The tapes contain interviews between Biden, now 83, and a ghostwriter working on his 2017 memoir, which was titled Promise Me, Dad. \"I just found all the classified stuff downstairs,\" Biden is heard saying at one point in the audio, recorded in February 2017, a month after he left the White House.A US Department of Justice (DOJ) special counsel found in 2024 that Biden had improperly retained records but did not recommend charging him. House Republicans and a conservative nonprofit, the Oversight Project, sued for the tapes as part of that probe into Biden's handling of the records.Responding to the release of the tapes, Biden's spokesman TJ Ducklo criticised the direction of the justice department under President Donald Trump - saying the move was \"just the latest example of this Administration weaponizing the DOJ for political retribution\". \"It's wrong, and while President Biden disagrees with today's decision, he also respects the courts and the vital role an independent judiciary plays in a healthy democracy,\" Ducklo added.The audio was recorded during the drafting of the 2017 memoir that Biden penned with co-writer Mark Zwonitzer. At one point in the more than two hours of tapes released on Monday, Biden tells the ghostwriter: \"The next thing I have here is, um, this is classified.\"In an October 2016 recording, he tells Zwonitzer: \"I have extensive notes over this period of time… They [the White House] didn't know I have this.\"At the time, Biden was still serving as vice-president to Barack Obama.During Biden's tenure as president from 2021-25, the justice department declined to release the audio tapes. In May, his personal lawyers sued the justice department in an effort to keep the tapes private, but after legal defeats they dropped their lawsuit.The recordings contain significant redactions, which Republicans on the House Oversight Committee say \"are intended to cover [Biden's] disclosure of classified information\".The recordings and transcripts were obtained by Special Counsel Robert Hur during his investigation in 2024 into Biden's handling of classified documents.Explaining his decision not recommend charges, Hur said he had concerns that a jury would take Biden's side and view him as \"a sympathetic, well-meaning, elderly man with a poor memory\".Trump was charged with illegally retaining the documents at his home in Florida, but the charges were dropped after he returned to the White House.",
      "source": "BBC",
