      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-24-waymo-reportedly-mulling-a-breakup-with-uber",
      "title": "Waymo reportedly mulling a breakup with Uber",
      "summary": "The contract between the two companies ends in May 2028, Uber told TechCrunch.",
      "body": "1:43 PM PDT · July 24, 2026\nImage Credits:Eric Thayer/Los Angeles Times / Getty Images\nWaymo is reportedly looking for a way out of its deal with Uber, which has made the Alphabet-owned company’s robotaxis available on the ride-hailing giant’s network in Austin and Atlanta, according to the Financial Times.\nWaymo already told Uber that it intends to offer robotaxis on its own app in those markets starting in January 2028 and alongside the existing offering, the ride-hail giant told TechCrunch on Friday. Uber said the contract with Waymo that covers Austin and Atlanta ends in May 2028. The two companies already split in Phoenix earlier this year, as TechCrunch first reported.\nWaymo didn’t immediately respond to a request for comment.\nThis all follows months of rising tensions between Waymo and Uber. Earlier this year, Uber CTO Praveen Neppalli posted a video of what he thought was unsafe and “scary” behavior of a Waymo robotaxi. In May, Uber CEO Dara Khosrowshahi lightly criticized the behavior of Waymo’s robotaxis in school zones and emergency situations during an earnings call, though without naming the company.\nWaymo, meanwhile, has wound up opposite Uber in a number of fresh policy fights over robotaxi regulations.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Sean O'Kane",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-24T20:43:35.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/waymo-back-getty.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/24/waymo-reportedly-mulling-a-breakup-with-uber/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
