      "id": "bbc-2026-07-24-2-000-pigeons-shot-with-rifles-at-railway-stations",
      "title": "2,000 pigeons shot with rifles at railway stations",
      "summary": "At York Station, 152 birds were shot in one day, a Freedom of Information Request has found.",
      "body": "Rick Pellen from Pest UK said it was important to keep pigeon numbers controlled so they were not causing hazards, but the method of shooting was typically a \"last resort\".\"Pigeons faeces carry many diseases such as psittacosis and salmonella, and when you get a lot of pigeons concentrated on a property and you get a build-up, it can be a serious health hazard,\" he said.\"The last time I did a pigeon cull in a train station, I think I euthanised about 55 birds.\"Rob Loft, of Yor-Pest Control, said he tended to \"steer clear\" of shooting the birds but \"in tricky places, for public health, it's got to be done\".\"I always feel sorry for something that's been shot,\" he added.\"I prefer to guide them away if possible, but expense is probably the problem, especially somewhere like York Station.\"Loft and Pellen each said they felt the birds had to be classified as vermin due to the spread of disease.Sue Joyce, director of the National Pigeon Advocacy Association, said: \"In theory, humans are a pest, dogs are a pest, cats are a pest, because they all carry diseases that can affect humans.\"She said the risks associated with their faeces gives pigeons \"a bad rap\", noting the birds themselves do not carry diseases.Humans domesticated the birds and used them as messengers and as food for thousands of years.They have only begun to be seen as pests relatively recently, after World War Two.\"What we've done is bred these animals, cast them aside and then given them the perfect environment...then we've given them these amazing tall buildings to perch in and to nest in,\" Joyce said.\"Pigeons are around us because we put them there.\"She said some birds do not die instantly when shot and instead get startled in the dark and collide with things, but Pellen said that was \"not true at all\".\"You just have to shoot them in the head,\" he said.He added that \"good pest controllers always, always kill them in a humane manner\".A Network Rail spokesperson said it was \"no secret\" that many railway stations provided \"attractive homes\" to the birds.They said a variety of deterrents, including spikes and vapour-releasing gel pods to discourage roosting, were used as a first port of call.\"A decision to cull pigeons is only taken as a last resort, where all other options have been exhausted,\" they added.As of December, Northern had stopped carrying out culls at stations, and has more recently stopped at its depots too.South Western Railway said it had not ordered any shootings since March 2026, and egg removal was carried out instead if possible.All state-owned operators in the UK were sent the request for information in April except Govia Thameslink Railway, which had not yet gone into public ownership.London Northwestern Railway and West Midlands Railway services were also only renationalised in February 2026.Transport for Wales said it did not hold data for cullings, other than 41 pigeons were shot at its stations in the final months of 2025.Transpennine Express and Southeastern have not responded to the request.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-24T05:20:19.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7004/live/368b5cd0-8737-11f1-a42c-83dd67bf1cb2.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyvnzpp914o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
