      "id": "techcrunch-2026-07-23-google-will-now-let-you-sign-in-to-your-account-with-a-selfi",
      "title": "Google will now let you sign in to your account with a selfie video",
      "summary": "The tech giant says selfie videos give users more options to sign in if they're ever locked out or don't have access to their usual phone or computer.",
      "body": "Image Credits:Jonathan Johnson/Bloomberg / Getty Images\n9:01 AM PDT · July 23, 2026\nGoogle is adding a selfie video option as a new way for users to log in to their accounts, the company announced on Thursday, joining a growing wave of tech companies betting that biometrics and not passwords are the future of identity verification online. The tech giant says selfie videos give users more options to sign in if they’re ever locked out or don’t have access to their usual phone or computer.\nUsers set up a selfie video by looking at their device’s camera and completing a few guided head movements — think turning right or left or nodding — to capture multiple angles of their face. If a user is having trouble signing in later, they can take another selfie video to get back into their account, and Google will then compare the new video to the one used at setup to confirm it’s the correct person.\nThe bigger challenge Google is trying to solve is proving a real human, not a bot or a doctored video, is on the other side of the camera. “When you use a selfie to sign in, we use multiple layers of security to help prevent impersonation attempts like fake photos and videos (i.e., deep fakes),” Google wrote in the blog post. “For example, we match your video against your saved selfie and require you to perform simple movements to prove it’s a live video. We also use our standard security practices to detect and help prevent suspicious sign-in attempts.”\nThis isn’t just about individual accounts, though. As AI-generated video gets more convincing, “liveness” checks are becoming a baseline requirement for any company handling logins, payments, or sensitive data.\nAlthough the new option could help protect accounts against fraud and help users recover locked accounts, it also raises concerns around user privacy and biometric data collection, an area regulators have increasingly scrutinized as more companies build products around facial and voice data.\nGoogle says selfie videos are stored securely using encryption and remain protected even when they’re not being used, and that users can choose to delete the videos from their Google account at any time.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAisha is a consumer news reporter at TechCrunch. Prior to joining the publication in 2021, she was a telecom reporter at MobileSyrup. Aisha holds an honours bachelor’s degree from University of Toronto and a master’s degree in journalism from Western University.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Aisha by emailing aisha@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at aisha_malik.01 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Aisha Malik",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-23T16:01:03.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/google-logo.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/23/google-will-now-let-you-sign-in-to-your-account-with-a-selfie-video/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-07-23-microsoft-is-testing-free-ad-supported-cloud-gaming-for-xbox",
      "title": "Microsoft is testing free, ad-supported cloud gaming for Xbox Insiders",
      "summary": "Xbox Insiders can stream games from their libraries for free in ad-supported streaming sessions starting today. The free streaming sessions are limited to games you already own, and sessions are capped at one hour. However, ads will only play \"before sessions begin.\" It's also optional - you won't need to watch ads if you have a paid cloud gaming subscription. \nMicrosoft says the ad-supported stre",
      "body": "Xbox Insiders can stream games from their libraries for free in ad-supported streaming sessions starting today. The free streaming sessions are limited to games you already own, and sessions are capped at one hour. However, ads will only play “before sessions begin.” It’s also optional — you won’t need to watch ads if you have a paid cloud gaming subscription.\nMicrosoft says the ad-supported streaming option will only be available for a limited time during a “test period.” It previously began internally testing an ad-supported version of Xbox Cloud Gaming late last year, with sources telling The Verge at the time that the test included about two minutes of preroll ads before streaming sessions.&nbsp;\nAround the same time, Microsoft also increased Game Pass prices, but added unlimited cloud gaming to its lowest-priced Game Pass Essential subscription, which costs $9.99 per month. Previously, you needed a Game Pass Ultimate subscription for Xbox Cloud Gaming. Microsoft says the goal of its current test for ad-supported streaming is to further expand affordable ways to play Xbox games. Ad tiers have become increasingly common on video streaming platforms over recent years — the addition of ads on Xbox’s streaming service could signal a similar shift coming to gaming subscriptions.&nbsp;\nAdditionally, Microsoft notes that cloud gaming could “help Xbox One owners play newer games built for Xbox Series X/S” without needing to buy a new console. The news comes as Xbox is in a state of upheaval, including mass layoffs, studio closures, and a pivot back toward the fan-favorite Fallout franchise. On top of that, Xbox console prices are set to increase by upwards of $100 starting on August 1st.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gaming",
        "Microsoft",
        "News",
        "Tech",
        "Xbox"
      ],
      "author": "Stevie Bonifield",
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-23T16:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/uploads/chorus_asset/file/25832866/STK048_XBOX_2_C.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/games/969969/xbox-testing-ad-supported-streaming",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-07-23-andy-burnham-pauses-prison-early-release-plan",
      "title": "Andy Burnham Pauses Prison Early Release Plan",
      "summary": "The Prime Minister orders an ‘urgent review’ into plan to release some prisoners early.",
      "body": "Home            News            Sport            Weather            iPlayer            Sounds            Bitesize     CBeebies     CBBC     Food         Home     News     Sport     Business     Technology     Health     Culture     Arts     Travel     Earth     Audio     Video     Live          Use BBC.com or the new BBC App to listen to BBC podcasts, Radio 4 and the World Service outside the UK.Find out how to listen to other BBC stationsEpisode detailsNews,·23 Jul 2026,·38 minsAndy Burnham Pauses Prison Early Release PlanNewscastAvailable for over a yearToday, Prime Minister Andy Burnham announced ‘no prisoners will be let out’ under early release scheme until he has conducted an ‘urgent review’.\nThe announcement comes after the widow of PC Andrew Harper described the possible early release of prisoners as ‘deplorable’, following reports that two of his killers could be freed early. Adam is joined by Dominic Casciani, home and legal correspondent, to discuss what this means for the prison system.\nPlus, in his third policy announcement in as many days, Andy Burnham announced a 20% cut to business rates for pubs, clubs and live music venues in England from April. Director of the Institute for Fiscal Studies Helen Miller and BBC Business Editor Simon Jack join Adam to discuss how the government plans to fund this cut and how ever-changing taxes affect business owners.\nYou can now listen to Newscast on a smart speaker. If you want to listen, just say \"Ask BBC Sounds to play Newscast”. It works on most smart speakers.\nYou can join our Newscast online community here: https://bbc.in/newscastdiscord\nGet in touch with Newscast by emailing newscast@bbc.co.uk or send us a WhatsApp on +44 0330 123 9480.\nNew episodes released every day. If you're in the UK, for more News and Current Affairs podcasts from the BBC, listen on BBC Sounds: https://bbc.in/4guXgXd\nNewscast brings you daily analysis of the latest political news stories from the BBC. The presenter was Adam Fleming. It was made by Jack Maclaren with Rosie Merotra.  The social producer was Joe Wilkinson. The technical producer was Phil Bull. The assistant editor is Chris Gray. The senior news editor is Sam Bonham.Programme WebsiteMore episodes",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-07-23T15:49:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/1024x576/p0l7jnbt.jpg",
